    "content": "A conflict between clans over grazing land in the Dawaco area of Somaliland's Sanag region has led to the displacement of 600 families and the deaths of seven people. The violence, which erupted on 25 July, has forced residents to flee to Badhan and other rural areas, where they are now living in precarious conditions without adequate shelter, food, or water.\n\nThe dispute, rooted in competition for scarce pasture and resources, comes at a time when the region is already suffering from prolonged drought. Abdi Askar Guled, chairman of Dawaco, noted that the fighting has exacerbated the economic difficulties communities were already facing due to recurrent dry spells. Livestock, a primary source of livelihood, have been lost or killed, and homes and property have been destroyed in the clashes.\n\nLocal authorities and elders have called for calm and have initiated mediation efforts to resolve the resource conflict. However, the displacement has strained host communities and humanitarian resources, with many families lacking basic necessities. The situation underscores the fragile intercommunal relations in the region, where climate change and resource scarcity are increasing tensions.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this event highlights the ongoing challenges of instability and climate vulnerability in the Horn of Africa. Many diaspora members have relatives among the displaced and are seeking ways to provide support. The crisis also serves as a reminder of the need for sustainable solutions that address both immediate humanitarian needs and long-term resource management to prevent similar conflicts.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/09/clan-conflict-over-resources-in-sanag-leaves-families-displaced-and-bereft/",
